      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic behavior without clear pattern or triggering condition could indeed be indicative of less obvious issues. Potential causes could include:\n1. Advanced network failures at the data link layer, including malfunctioning network interface cards (NICs) or problems with network transceivers.\n2. Software bugs or vulnerabilities in the firmware or OS running on the routers/stations, especially if they're sporadic and not consistently reproducible.\n3. Issues related to Quality of Service (QoS) settings or other network traffic management policies that may intermittently be affecting the traffic.\n4. Electromagnetic interference (EMI) from nearby equipment or natural phenomena.\n5. Sporadic connectivity issues resulting from transient electrical instabilities (brownouts).\n\nInvestigating these possibilities could definitely narrow down the cause. Running checksum tests would still be relevant to ensure the issue isn't due to packet corruption. Also, consider engaging with the router vendors, as this behavior might be a known or emerging issue they'd be aware of and potentially have a solution. Have you observed if the behavior correlates with certain types of traffic or network activity?",
